                <td>
                  <table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="3">
                    <tr> 
                      <td bgcolor="#000022" class="text"><span style="font-size: 14;font-weight: bold;">Vex Thal ... and other news</span><a href="http://www.webwizguide.info"></a></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr> 
                      
     <td bgcolor="414f6e" class="text"><a href="http://www.webwizguide.info"></a> 
      <table width="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
       <tr>
        <td class="text">Nostalgic visit to Luclin_timesink_01 aka Vex Thal for the usual dose of fun, frolics and loot. Pretty much a ho-hum raid, so I won't bore everyone with the gory details.<BR><BR>Instead, I want to talk about an article from the Los Angeles Business Journal written by Laura Dunphy back in March of 2000. The title of the article was "Loyalty? What's That?"  The original article is <A href="http://www.findarticles.com/cf_dls/m5072/13_22/61645158/p1/article.jhtml" target=_blank>here</A> if you're interested in reading it. I have modified the article's wording ever so slightly to apply to the world of Norrath, so with apologies to Ms Dunphy ...<BR><BR><br><BLOCKQUOTE><FONT color=cyan>The first day in a new guild has always brought lots of expectations to fulfil and new people to meet.<BR><BR>These days, it can even bring dreams of moving on to an even more uber guild.<BR><BR>OK, the turnaround might not be that sudden, but the growing number of guilds with access to the Plane of Time has resulted in frequent guild-hopping, with loot and supposedly the "lure of a new challenge" as the driving force.<BR><BR>Tagging with a new guild with Plane of Time access can increase the possibility of "Phat Lewtz" immeasurably meaning loyalty isn't what it used to be.<BR><BR>"Traditional terms of loyalty have changed. What you find are guildmates who are much more loyal to their own advancement and their marketability than to a guild," said an anonymous ex-officer of Vis Maior who secretly transferred servers in order to join a guild with Plane of Time access behind everyone's back.  "There are very, very strong toons who have had three to five changes in three years. The rewards, both in terms of loot and perceived prestige are quite compelling today."<BR><BR>"Many people change guilds because their current guild have hit a bump in the road, and cannot immediately overcome the major flag obstacles that could easily be surpassed by leaving for another guild, such as Afterlife, Enlightened Dark or Township Rebellion", said an anonymous member of Vis Maior. "The sense of betrayal caused by these people leaving has an damaging effect on morale. Although my contention is that by guild-hopping in order to advance, these people have shown that they don't have the guts or the desire to work for their rewards. They want things handed to them by someone else. From my perspective, we're better off without them."<BR><BR>"I've seen guilds take 2 months to achieve a major kill and be very proud of their achievement," said a former guild leader of Vis Maior. "Meanwhile, the guild-hoppers like myself are already two kills ahead by moving around to whichever guild(s) have the hot hand. You tell me which situation you would rather be in."<BR><BR>To combat the exodus caused by the lure of "Phat Lewtz" and perceived "Uberness" in the eyes of other players, many guilds are re-focusing their priorities on building a solid core and continually emphasizing the tradition and teamwork that are the hallmarks of a truly successful guild, though they might not be as compelling as insta-access to the Plane of Time.<BR><BR>Though fairly frequent guild changes are becoming a given in today's game, all is not lost for guilds who want loyal, passionate players, especially for tradition-rich guilds that have hit a minor bump in the road.  The strength of these guilds lies not in sheer numbers, nor in the amount of loot acquired, nor even in their progression or advancement through the game.  Rather their strength lies in the dedication and commitment of its loyal members - those committed to strive for excellence, even in the face of adversity and hardship.</BLOCKQUOTE></FONT><br>And for those people at whom this is targeted (and who might be a bit slow on the uptake), I have four words for you: Honor, Family, Loyalty, Honesty. Go look them up sometime. Then go look in the mirror. </td>
       </tr>
       <tr> 
        <td align="right" class="smItalic" height="20">Posted by <a href="mailto:" style="font-size: 11px; font-style: italic;"> 
